\DOC SPLIT_FIRST_CONJ_ASSUM_TAC

\TYPE {SPLIT_FIRST_CONJ_ASSUM_TAC : tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Finds an assumption that is a conjunction and replaces it by its two halves,
leaving the conclusion unchanged.

\DESCRIBE
Takes no arguments. Applied to a goal {A ?- t}, it selects one assumption of
the form {u1 /\ u2}, discards it and adds {u1} and {u2} as separate
assumptions:
{
   A, u1 /\ u2 ?- t
   ========================  SPLIT_FIRST_CONJ_ASSUM_TAC
   A, u1, u2 ?- t
}
\noindent Thus a right-nested conjunction loses only its outermost conjunct,
the remaining conjunction staying as a single assumption, so repeated
applications peel conjuncts one at a time. The new assumption list is reordered
as HOL Light's assumption insertion dictates, so the two new assumptions need
not sit where the original did.

\FAILURE
Fails, reporting a search failure, when none of the goal's assumptions is a
conjunction — including goals whose assumptions are all atomic and goals with
no assumptions at all; a conjunctive or disjunctive conclusion does not help.

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, always
leaving one subgoal and never closing a goal.

\ENDDOC
